  "archive_id": "BW2-B57-P03",
  "title": "Polynomial Solvability of a Two-Machine Unit-Time Resource-Constrained Job Shop with at Most Three Operations per Job",
  "batch_label": "57",
  "kind": "resolution-paper",
  "original_status": "proved",
  "scope": "For every finite instance with two dedicated machines, nonpreemptive unit-time operations, at most three operations per job in a prescribed route whose consecutive operations use different machines, one unit-capacity auxiliary resource, and a job-level demand bit in {0,1} shared by all operations of that job, an optimal makespan schedule can be computed in polynomial time. Specifically, shortest-path dynamic programming over counts of the twelve (route, demand) types at each completed prefix has at most (n+3)^24 states and exactly represents all feasible schedules after an integral-slot normalization. Thus J2 | res^t 111, p_ij=1, m_j<=3 | C_max is polynomial-time solvable under the frozen reading in problem.md. Zero-demand jobs are legal under the cited printed definition; if a nonempty instance is additionally required to contain a demand-one job, marking one existing job as the sole demand-one job leaves the resource constraint vacuous and preserves every feasible schedule and makespan.",
  "gate_basis": "Current released terminal, manifest, PDF, TeX freeze, snapshot files, provenance and four audits agree.",
